#{[17592186114940 :question/worked-solution "We'll use proof by contradiction, assuming that such a graph can exist.\n\n> Suppose that graph $G$ with these properties exists, and let $V$ and $E$ be the corresponding sets of vertices and edges. Then, according to the degree sum formula for graphs, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = \\bbox[]{\\color{black}2} \\cdot \\big \\vert \\bbox[]{\\color{black}E} \\big \\vert.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to reach a contradiction. First, we'll show that the left-hand side of the above expression is odd by considering the sum under the sigma notation and analyzing the rest of the expression separately.\n\n> Let the vertices $v_7$, $v_9$, and $v_{11}$ have degrees $7$, $9$, and $11$, respectively, and let $W$ be the set of all vertices in $V$ with even degrees. Now, we can expand the left-hand side as\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = \\sum_{v \\in W} \\deg(v) + \\deg(v_7) + \\deg(v_9) + \\deg(v_{11}).\n$$\n> Notice that\n> $\\displaystyle \\sum_{v \\in W} \\deg(v)$\n> must be even because the sum of even numbers is even.\n\nNow, we examine the second part of the sum on the right-hand side and draw a conclusion about the entire expression.\n\n> Since $\\deg(v_7) + \\deg(v_9) + \\deg(v_{11}) = \\bbox[]{\\color{black}27}$, adding this to the even sum $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{v \\in W} \\deg(v)$ results in an odd total for the left-hand side.\n\nNext, we deduce that the other side of the formula is even.\n\n> On the other hand, the right-hand side of our degree sum formula is even as it is a multiple of $2$.\n\nWe have thus obtained a contradiction: an even number must equal an odd number, which is impossible. Hence, we conclude the following:\n\n> Therefore, the graph described above cannot exist.\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Suppose that graph $G$ with these properties exists, and let $V$ and $E$ be the corresponding sets of vertices and edges. Then, according to the degree sum formula for graphs, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = \\bbox[]{\\color{black}2} \\cdot \\big \\vert \\bbox[]{\\color{black}E} \\big \\vert.\n$$\n> Let the vertices $v_7$, $v_9$, and $v_{11}$ have degrees $7$, $9$, and $11$, respectively, and let $W$ be the set of all vertices in $V$ with even degrees. Now, we can expand the left-hand side as\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{v \\in V} \\deg(v) = \\sum_{v \\in W} \\deg(v) + \\deg(v_7) + \\deg(v_9) + \\deg(v_{11}).\n$$\n> Notice that\n> $\\displaystyle \\sum_{v \\in W} \\deg(v)$\n> must be even because the sum of even numbers is even.\n> Since $\\deg(v_7) + \\deg(v_9) + \\deg(v_{11}) = \\bbox[]{\\color{black}27}$, the complete expression on the left-hand side must be odd because the sum of an even number and an odd number is odd.\n> On the other hand, the right-hand side of our degree sum formula is even as it is a multiple of $2$.\n> Therefore, the graph described above cannot exist."] [17592186171723 :question/problem "Given that $\\big \\vert (-4,4) \\big \\vert = \\mathfrak{c}$, what are the missing entries in the following reasoning regarding the cardinality of the set $[-4,4]$?\n\n> Since the function $f: x \\to x$ (that maps any number to itself) is an injection from $(-4,4)$ into $[-4,4]$, and\n> $g: x \\to \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\cdot x$\n> is an injection from $[-4,4]$ **onto** $[-2,2] \\subseteq (-4,4)$, according to Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have that\n> $\\big \\vert [-4,4] \\big \\vert = \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAA}}$."] [17592186171726 :question/problem "Given that $A = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\,: \\, x^2 <4 \\}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\big \\vert \\mathcal{P}(A)\\big \\vert > \\aleph_0$\n2. $\\big \\vert \\mathcal{P}(A)\\big \\vert > \\mathfrak{c}$\n3. $\\big \\vert \\mathcal{P}(A)\\big \\vert = 2^{\\mathfrak{c}}$"] [17592186171727 :question/problem "Given that $\\mid \\mathbb{R}|=\\mathfrak{c}$, what are the missing entries in the following reasoning regarding the cardinality of the set $(-\\infty,-4) \\cup [1,\\infty)$?\n\n> Since the function $f: x \\to x$ (that maps any number to itself) is an injection from $(-\\infty,-4) \\cup [1,\\infty)$ into $\\mathbb R$, and\n> $$\n\\displaystyle g: x \\to \\dfrac{2}{\\pi} \\, \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}} + \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\n$$\n> is an injection from $\\mathbb{R}$ **onto** $(1,3) \\subseteq (-\\infty,-4) \\cup [1,\\infty)$, according to Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have that\n> $\\big \\vert (-\\infty,-4) \\cup [1,\\infty) \\big \\vert = \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAA}}$."] [17592186171728 :question/problem "Given that $\\big \\vert \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R} \\big \\vert = \\mathfrak{c}$, what are the missing entries in the reasoning below regarding the cardinality of the set $\\mathbb R\\times [-2,3]$.\n\n> Since the function $f: (x,y) \\to (x, y)$ (that maps any ordered pair to itself) is an injection from $\\mathbb{R} \\times [-2,3]$ into $\\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R}$, and\n> $g: (x,y) \\to \\bigg(x, \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}} \\bigg)$\n> is an injection from $\\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R}$ **onto** $\\mathbb{R} \\times (-1,1) \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\times [-2,3]$, according to Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have that\n> $\\big \\vert \\mathbb{R} \\times [-2,3] \\big \\vert = \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAA}}$."] [17592186192393 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the definite integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating our integral using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.75\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186192394 :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot our region using a graphing calculator.\n\nTo get a better view, we might need to either\n\n- zoom in or out, or\n- adjust the window ranges.\n\nWe can adjust the window settings using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{window}\\,}$ button (or equivalent).\n\nIn our case, the appropriate window ranges could be the following:\n\n- for the horizontal axis, $x \\in [-2.5,2.5]$\n- for the vertical axis, $y \\in [-3,1]$\n\nThis gives us the following graph:\n\n![](images/b2/b2675f8f7be370b26a070c686a72b17861e598ff33cfb1d7d0ae6f13eaab9d72.png)\n\nAccording to the graph, the lower limit corresponds to the root of the function $f(x)$ that lies in the interval $x \\in [-2,0]$.\n\nUsing a graphing calculator, we find that this root, rounded to $3$ decimal places, is\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\na \\approx -1.034\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral with the lower limit $a=-1.034{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-1.034}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating our integral using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-1.034}^{1.312} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx -3.934\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186192417 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert *increasingly large* numbers in place of $\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with upper limit $9$ using a calculator, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{9} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.225},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with upper limit $99$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{99} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.225},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $0.225$ to $3$ decimal places.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\sqrt{2x}e^{-x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.225\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186192418 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}}\\,}\n$$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert decreasingly small numbers in place of $-\\infty$ and increasingly large numbers in place of $\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-9$ and upper limit $9$ using a calculator, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-9}^{9} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.73},\n$$\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-99$ and upper limit $99$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-99}^{99} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{0.73},\n$$\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $0.73$, to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{x^4 + 2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 0.73\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186192507 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) button, then the $\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}}\\,}$ button.\n- Press the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and choose the integral option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n- To access the absolute value function, you can locate this function on your calculator (on some models, you can access this by typing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ and then selecting $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{abs}\\,}$ under the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{num}\\,}$ submenu). Or, you can recall that $\\mid x \\mid = \\sqrt{x^2}$ and rewrite the integral as\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{(\\sqrt{x^2})^3}{2^{-x} + x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo calculate our improper integral, we insert *decreasingly small* numbers in place of $-\\infty$ until successive approximations are the same, as follows:\n\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-10$ using a calculator, we have\n$$\n\\int_{-10}^{0}  \\dfrac{|x|^3}{2^{-x} + x}  \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 30.954,\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-100$ using a calculator, we get\n$$\n\\int_{-100}^{0}  \\dfrac{|x|^3}{2^{-x} + x}  \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{33.183},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n- Evaluating the integral with lower limit $-200$ using a calculator, we obtain\n$$\n\\int_{-200}^{0}  \\dfrac{|x|^3}{2^{-x} + x}  \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{33.183},\n$$\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nWe can stop now since the last two approximations were $33.18$ to $2$ decimal places.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0}  \\dfrac{|x|^3}{2^{-x} + x}  \\, \\textrm{d}x \\approx 33.18,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186193818 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nA sketch of this function is shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1fd56fbb40743ccf15288f79b5847bbd990a239ce8a2cb4fa7f1974d914edc3.png)\n\nThe finite area $\\mathcal A$ bounded by $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's use our graphing calculator to approximate this area. Since the function we want to integrate contains trigonometric functions, your calculator *must* be in radians mode!\n\nWe bring up the definite integral option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n1. Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\displaystyle\\color{gray}\\,\\displaystyle \\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}}\\,}\n$$ button. You may need to press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,2\\text{nd}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{shift}\\,}$) first.\n2. Using the $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{math}\\,}$ (or $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{calc}\\,}$) button and selecting the \"integration\" option. Note that this might be called $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\text{fnInt}\\,}$ or similar.\n\nThe next steps depend on whether your calculator uses derivative or function notation. We'll discuss both. You're encouraged to read both instructions as there are several parallels.\n\nIntegral Notation\n\nIf your calculator uses derivative notation, you should see a prompt that looks similar to the following:\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we fill in the empty boxes as follows:\n\n- Enter $0$ for the lower integration limit. Then, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$ to move the cursor to the upper integration limit, and enter the value $\\pi$.The prompt should now look as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n- Navigate the cursor to the next empty box by pressing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$. Enter the function definition by pressing the following keys:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}} \\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\sin\\,}\\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n} \\quad\\:\\: \\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,)\\,}\n$$\nThe prompt should now look as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin(x)} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n- Navigate the cursor to the next empty box by pressing $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\blacktriangleright\\,}$. Press $\\bbox[3px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}X,T,\\theta,n}$ to enter the variable that we're integrating with respect to.The prompt should now look as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin(x)} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n- Finally, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\textrm{enter}\\,}$, and the calculator will return the numerical value of the integral.\n\nFollowing the above steps, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\\approx 2.396\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFunction Notation\n\nIf your calculator uses function notation, then instead of being presented with\n\n$$\n\\int_{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}^{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}} \\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{\\big \\vert 0\\big \\vert}} \\,\\textrm{d}\\,{\\bbox[gray]{\\,\\phantom{0}}}\n$$\n\nyou'll instead see a prompt that looks similar to the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{integral}\\Big(\\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}\\text{expression}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}\\text{variable}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}\\text{lowerBound}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}}, \\bbox[gray]{\\color{white}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}\\text{upperBound}\\phantom{\\Big \\vert}}\\Big)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we place the function definition in the first box, the variable in the second box, and the lower and upper limits in the third and fourth boxes, respectively.\n\nBy entering this information, the prompt will look as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{integral}\\Big(\\,\\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,, \\,x\\,, \\,0\\,, \\,\\pi\\, \\Big)\n$$\n\nOnce you're done, press $\\bbox[2px, border: 2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}\\,\\textrm{enter}\\,}$.\n\nFollowing the above steps, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\sin x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\\approx 2.396\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- It's important to remember that the graphing calculator finds a numerical *approximation* of the definite integral.\n- Moreover, different calculators use different algorithms to arrive at their approximations.\n- For that reason, different calculators may give slightly different answers when approximating the definite integral of a function.\n- Despite this, the answers returned by different calculators will agree when the answer is rounded to two or three decimal places in most cases."] [17592186199556 :tutorial/content "The Cartesian product of two countably infinite sets is countably infinite. Furthermore, this property can be generalized to any *finite* number of factors:\n\n> *The Cartesian product of a finite number of countably infinite sets is countably infinite.*\n\nWe can express this as follows:\n\n> *If $\\mid A_i \\mid = \\aleph_0$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$, then $\\displaystyle \\big \\vert A_1 \\times A_2 \\times \\cdots \\times A_n \\big \\vert = \\aleph_0$.*\n\nLet's demonstrate why this is true for the case of two factors, $A$ and $B$.\n\n- Since $A$ is countably infinite, it can be represented as a (one-way infinite) sequence, as illustrated below.\n\n![](images/ad/adaf378c1864423563da6bb64d3194ad83e4b0059f381a99de765a67b37e2ef9.png)\n\n- Similarly, since $B$ is countably infinite, it can also be represented as a (one-way infinite) sequence.\n\n![](images/12/12f968dfbcbcc69056ae0b1ab27c44b75e13720aeb884621087a26428005cf4d.png)\n\nTherefore, we can view the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ as an infinite array that extends forever to the right and down, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f7ea0c73c14246531b018c5238b796751c8c271e9e26093b7505f6b4e1160ad.png)\n\nThis array can be traversed as follows:\n\n![](images/98/98ed3b518e480f11e6676432c9db323dfda6893725ad72ce11549d1cfaf45484.png)\n\nAs a result, we get a bijection between $A \\times B$ and the set of natural numbers $\\mathbb{N}$."] [17592186199561 :tutorial/content "Recall that any infinite subset of a countably infinite set is countably infinite. So, we have the following result concerning intersections of countably infinite sets:\n\n> *The intersection of two countably infinite sets is either finite or countably infinite.*\n\nWe also have the following result concerning unions of countably infinite sets:\n\n> *The union of two countably infinite sets is countably infinite.*\n\nThis property can be generalized to the union of any number of countably infinite sets and even to the union of *countably many* countably infinite sets:\n\n> *The union of countably many countably infinite sets is countably infinite.*\n\nWe can express this result as follows:\n\n> *If $\\mid A_i \\mid = \\aleph_0$ for all natural $i$, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\bigg \\vert \\bigcup_{i=1}^\\infty A_i \\bigg \\vert = \\aleph_0.\n$$*\n\nIf we combine \"fewer\" sets (e.g., a finite number), or if some of the $A_i$'s are finite, the resulting union must also be countable."] [17592186208402 :question/problem "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=49 \\\\ y &=x^{2} - 2x + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What is the possible value of $x$?"] [17592186221018 :question/worked-solution "Recall that each equation of a linear system defines a straight line. The solution to the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nLet’s find the equations of the lines.\n\n- For the first (orange) line, we pick two points on the line.\nFor example, this line passes through $(0, -5)$ and $(1, -3)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\frac{-3 - (-5)}{1 - 0} = \\frac{2}{1} = 2\n$$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, -5)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y + 5|= 2(x - 0); y|= 2x - 5\n$$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|= 2x - 5; - 2x + y|=-5\n$$\n- For the second (blue) line, we pick two points on the line.\nThis line passes through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, -1)$.Now, we compute the slope:\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\frac{-1 - 0}{2 - 0} = -\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\nUsing the slope and the point $(0, 0)$, we can write the equation in slope-point form:\n$y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand rewrite it in standard form:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y|=-\\frac{1}{2}x; 2y|=-x; x + 2y|= 0\n$$\n\nSo, the system that matches the graph is the following:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x + y &=-5 \\\\ x + 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only I.**"] [17592186221019 :question/worked-solution "The solution to a system of two linear equations corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nBy inspecting the graph, we can see that the lines intersect at the point $(1, -4)$.\n\nSo, the solution can be written as follows:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only III.**"] [17592186221068 :question/problem "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x - 15y &=25 \\\\ - x - 3y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nHow many solutions does the given system of equations have?"] [17592186221094 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent parallel lines that never intersect. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet’s analyze each system:\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-3 \\\\ y &=x - 3\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nThe first equation is a horizontal line, so it has slope $0$; the second has slope $1$. The slopes are different, so the lines intersect at one point.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has one solution.\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2x + 3 \\\\ y &=2x - 5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nThe slopes are different ($-2$ and $2$), so the lines intersect at one point.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has one solution.\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=6 \\\\ y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nThis system contains a vertical line and a horizontal line. These lines intersect at exactly one point.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has one solution.\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=5x + 9 \\\\ y &=5x - 4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations have slope $5$, but different $y$-intercepts ($9$ and $-4$). These lines are parallel and distinct, so they never intersect.\n\n$\\qquad$ Thus, this system has **no** solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only II.**"] [17592186224903 :question/problem "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=3x - 5 \\\\ y &=-2x^{2} + 3x + 3\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nIf $(x,y)$ is a solution to the system of equations given above, what is the smallest possible value of $x$?"] [17592186225016 :question/problem "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=13 \\\\ y &=\\mid - 3x + 4 \\mid\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations in the $xy$-plane intersect at the point $(x, y)$. What are the possible values of $x$?"] [17592186225016 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following system:\n\n$$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=13 \\\\ y &=\\mid - 3x + 4 \\mid\\end{aligned}\\right.$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we know that $y = 13$.\n\nSubstituting $y = 13$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\mid -3x + 4 \\mid = 13\n$$\n\nThis absolute value equation has two cases:\n\n- If $-3x + 4 > 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle -3x + 4|= 13; - 3x|= 9; x|=-3.\n$$\n- If $-3x + 4 < 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle -3x + 4|=-13; - 3x|=-17; x|= \\frac{17}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = -3$ and\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{17}{3}\n$$\n\nare the two solutions of the given system of equations."] [17592186225017 :question/problem "$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=9 \\\\ y &=4 \\cdot 2^{x - 2} + 1\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThe graphs of the given equations intersect at the point $(x, y)$ in the $xy$-plane. What is the value of $y$?"] [17592186233695 :question/worked-solution "The angles ${0}^{∘}$ and ${180}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}({180}^{∘})=-1.$  Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${0}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${0}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})=0.$  Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}({0}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})$ is undefined.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})$ is undefined, we conclude that the expression $\\operatorname{sec}({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}({0}^{∘})$ is undefined."] [17592186660347 :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-{e}^{-y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({e}^{-y})\\cdot |-{e}^{-y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({e}^{-y})^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2y}}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660350 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=36$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{x-36}{\\sqrt{x}-6} & =\\frac{36-36}{\\sqrt{36}-6}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{x-36}{\\sqrt{x}-6} & =\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{(x-36)(\\sqrt{x}+6)}{(\\sqrt{x}-6)(\\sqrt{x}+6)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{(x-36)(\\sqrt{x}+6)}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{(x-36)(\\sqrt{x}+6)}{x-36} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-36)}(\\sqrt{x}+6)}{\\cancel{x-36}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 36}(\\sqrt{x}+6) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36}+6 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660362 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{{2}^{n}n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{n+1}(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}n!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-{x}^{2}n!}{2(n+1)!}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|-{x}^{2}|}{2}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n!}{(n+1)n!} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [17592186660370 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(n+1)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+2}}{(n+2)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-{x}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}{(n+2)^{2}}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n+2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $|x|<1.$ So, the radius of convergence is $1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=1$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(n+1)^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{(1)}^{2n}}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{2}}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=1$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(n+1)^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{(-1)}^{2n}}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{2}}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test. So the endpoint $x=-1$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-1,1].$"] [17592186660371 :question/worked-solution "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=X-1.$ The transformation $u(x)=x-1$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =y-1 \\\\ y & ={u}^{-1}(y)-1 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=1\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(y+1)\\cdot |1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}, & y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660887 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration. \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{16}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{16}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{16}3{x}^{-1/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}4{x}^{3/4}{|}_{a}^{16} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}{|}_{a}^{16} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[4\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{{16}^{3}}-4\\cdot \\sqrt[4]{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & =4(8)-4(0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186666068 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186666135 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =2+2=2\\cdot 2 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =2\\cdot 2+2=3\\cdot 2 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =3\\cdot 2+2=4\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $1\\le n\\le 10$ is ${s}_{n}=2n.$ In particular, ${s}_{10}=2\\cdot 10=20.$\n\nLet's compute the first few partial sums for $n\\ge 11:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{11} & =20+0=20 \\\\ {s}_{12} & =20+0=20 \\\\ {s}_{13} & =20+0=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the sequence of partial sums, we can see that the formula for the $n$th partial sum for $n\\ge 11$ is\n\n$$\n{s}_{n}=20.\n$$\n\nThe sequence of partial sums is convergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{s}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }20=20\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converges to $20.$"] [17592186667701 :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true, $f(x)$ is continuous and differentiable everywhere except, possibly, at $x=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  Let's  investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at $x=2.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{2}-4)={2}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}-4)={2}^{2}-4=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(2)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2$ (it is a removable discontinuity). Therefore,  $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2$ (Since differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true.   Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=2.$   Since $x=2$  doesn't belong to $(-2,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."]}
